  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1025 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe majority of Tuesday is also in the transitional period\nawaiting the next weather maker. Dual low pressure systems are \nslated to eject from the Rockies on Tuesday morning: one with a \ntrajectory over the Great Lakes and another with a track along the\nI-40 corridor. The southernmost surface low will be more \nimpactful for Middle TN, but it looks like we are in the sweet \nspot in between favorable severe weather ingredients. The Great \nLakes system will be displaced too far north to cause any impacts.\nForecast surface analyses depict the southern system deepening to\nthe point of occlusion by the time it reaches the Mississippi \nRiver on Wednesday morning, centered over west TN. This will help \nkeep the axis of highest instability confined to the Gulf Coast \nduring FROPA and limit our severe weather potential. Again, the \nmain impact from this next system will be another good soaking for\nMiddle TN. Storm total QPF probabilities of rainfall > 2\" are \nabout 50-60% per the LREF next week. \n\nAs the occluded low migrates off to the east on Thursday and \nFriday, wraparound showers from this same system will continue to \nimpact parts of Middle TN for several days. Broad upper level \ntroughing per the LREF supports much cooler temperatures (finally \nnear normal in the mid 60s) and 20-40% PoPs through at least \nFriday morning. We finally dry back out on Friday evening but \nremain cool.",
